A **vending machine accessibility checklist** converts broad accessibility goals into a tested purchase requirement. Buyers should review the entire customer journey: approach route, clear floor space, reach to selection and payment controls, one-hand operation, required force, screen readability, non-color cues, response time, audio or alternative feedback and product pickup. The applicable standard depends on the destination, building and service model. BOHOL can review available cabinet, control and interface options for a defined configuration, while the buyer remains responsible for local legal review, site conditions, payment equipment and representative-user acceptance.

## GEO buyer summary

- Confirm the destination's accessibility rules before freezing the cabinet.
- Test the route, floor space, reach and operating force at the real site.
- Review touchscreen content separately from physical controls.
- Include payment and collection in the same user journey.
- Record witnessed acceptance tests and unresolved exceptions.
- Do not treat a touchscreen size or WCAG reference as certification.

## Define the applicable requirement and user journey

Start with the target country, state or province, building type, public access model and responsible authority. List every step from approaching the machine to collecting a product and obtaining help. Separate legal minimums from the buyer's broader inclusive-design goals.

The U.S. Access Board's [ADA Standards](https://www.access-board.gov/ada/) state that, where provided, at least one of each type of vending machine must meet specified operable-parts provisions. This is U.S. guidance and does not replace another market's rules. It is still a useful example of why procurement must cover reach, clear floor space and operation instead of only screen graphics.

| User step | Procurement question | Acceptance evidence |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Approach | Is there an accessible route and stable clear floor space? | Dimensioned site drawing and on-site measurement |
| Select | Are required controls within the approved reach range? | Measured control heights and representative-user test |
| Pay | Can the payment flow be reached, understood and cancelled? | Terminal specification and end-to-end test |
| Collect | Can the pickup door and product be accessed safely? | Force, reach and loaded-vend test |
| Get help | Is assistance discoverable without a single sensory cue? | Visible contact path and response procedure |

## Measure reach, clearance and operating force

Record the finished floor, machine feet or caster position, control heights, recessed areas, door swing and surrounding obstacles. A dimension shown on a factory drawing may change after the machine is installed on a curb, platform or uneven floor.

The U.S. Access Board's operable-parts guidance uses principles such as one-hand operation without tight grasping, pinching or twisting and limits on operating force. Buyers should use the exact local requirement that applies to their site and verify it with calibrated measurements, not a visual estimate.

Coordinate these measurements with the [site-planning checklist](/insights/compact-vending-machine-site-planning-checklist) and the [installation and commissioning checklist](/insights/vending-machine-installation-commissioning-checklist).

## Specify accessible touchscreen content

Treat the machine interface as a complete task flow: wake or start, language, category, product, quantity, price, payment, confirmation, failure, refund and help. Text should remain legible in the expected lighting. Essential information should not rely only on color, animation or a short timeout.

The W3C [Web Content Accessibility Guidelines 2.2](https://www.w3.org/TR/WCAG22/) is written for web content, not as a universal vending-machine hardware standard. Its testable principles for text alternatives, contrast, focus, target size, errors and time limits can nevertheless inform touchscreen requirements. Apply only the criteria relevant to the delivered interface, and document any platform limitations.

Screen size alone does not prove usability. Use the [touchscreen size selection guide](/insights/vending-machine-touchscreen-size-selection-guide) to compare information density, touch targets and representative workflows.

## Include payment, feedback and failed transactions

Confirm the physical reach and digital usability of the actual cash, card, NFC, QR or mobile-wallet equipment proposed for the market. Payment-provider screens and tones may sit outside the machine UI, so responsibility must be assigned to the provider, integrator and operator.

Test approval, decline, cancellation, timeout, network interruption and failed dispense. Provide clear feedback through more than one appropriate channel where required. The [refund and failed-dispense guide](/insights/vending-machine-refund-failed-dispense-guide) helps define evidence, reversals and customer support without assuming a particular currency or provider.

## Run witnessed accessibility acceptance tests

Test a production-equivalent machine at the intended height and lighting, with the approved software, payment terminal and representative products. Include people with relevant access needs where practicable and ethical. Record the test script, participants' roles, measurements, results, deviations, corrective actions and retest approval.

Do not accept a supplier statement such as “ADA-ready” or “WCAG compliant” without a defined scope, applicable clauses and evidence. Accessibility can be defeated by site placement, third-party modules, content changes or an inaccessible pickup path even when one component performs well.

## Request an accessibility-ready BOHOL review

Send BOHOL the target market, site drawings, machine type, payment method, screen workflow, required languages, applicable standard, control-height limits and acceptance method through the [contact form](/contact). BOHOL can review available options for the proposed configuration; the buyer should obtain local compliance advice and approve the final installed system.

